According to a new report from Intel Market Research, the global Aerospace APU market was valued at USD 5.2 billion in 2025 and is projected to reach USD 7.9 billion by 2034, growing at a robust CAGR of 4.3% during the forecast period (2026–2034). This growth is propelled by the rising penetration of modern, fuel efficient power units, tightening airport side emissions regulations, and the increasing adoption of predictive maintenance capabilities across both commercial and military fleets.
Auxiliary Power Units (APUs) are self contained turbine generator sets that supply electricity and pneumatic pressure when an aircraft’s main engines are offline. They enable cabin climate control, engine start up, and the operation of onboard systems without external ground support, making them essential for the efficiency and reliability of air transport operations worldwide.
